# Copy to `.dev.vars` and fill in. Never commit real values.
#
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
#
# This one file covers both halves of the project:
#
#   * Worker secrets      — loaded by `wrangler dev` as runtime bindings, and set
#                           in production with `./scripts/cf.sh secrets`.
#   * Deploy credentials  — read by ./scripts/cf.sh to pin which Cloudflare
#                           account every wrangler command targets.
#
# Note: `wrangler dev` injects everything here into the Worker's `env`, so the
# CLOUDFLARE_* values are visible to local dev code. They are NOT present in
# production — deployed secrets come only from `wrangler secret put`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Worker secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The bot's Bluesky account, and an App Password (NOT the account password).
# Generate at: Settings -> Privacy and Security -> App Passwords
BLUESKY_HANDLE="your-bot.bsky.social"
BLUESKY_APP_PASSWORD="xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx"

# Shared secret guarding the dashboard and the manual /run/* routes (?key=...).
# Also used by `./scripts/cf.sh seed` to call those routes.
MANUAL_TRIGGER_KEY="choose-a-long-random-string"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare deploy credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Setting a token is what pins the account: with several Cloudflare accounts a
# cached `wrangler login` session is ambiguous, and a deploy can land in the wrong
# one. ./scripts/cf.sh exports these and verifies them before anything mutating.
#
# For more than one account, keep a file per account and select it with -p:
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars.personal
#   ./scripts/cf.sh -p personal deploy

# Find it on any domain's Overview page in the dashboard, or list what a token
# can see:
#   curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN" \
#     https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/accounts
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID="your-cloudflare-account-id"

# Create at https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens with these
# permissions on the target account:
#   Account · Workers Scripts    · Edit
#   Account · Workers KV Storage · Edit
#   Account · D1                 · Edit
#   Account · Vectorize          · Edit
#   Account · Workers AI         · Read
#
# May also be a 1Password reference, resolved with `op read` at runtime so no
# token sits on disk:
#   CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN="op://Private/Cloudflare grunge/credential"
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN="your-cloudflare-api-token"
